    topic: "A driverless shuttle trial",
    passage: "When the city council of Samarkand announced that a driverless shuttle would carry passengers along a two-kilometre route near the Registan, most residents reacted with curiosity rather than alarm. The trial, which began on the fourteenth of March and was scheduled to last ninety days, was organised by an engineering firm led by Dilnoza Rakhimova, who had spent eleven years working on navigation software before moving back to Uzbekistan. Contrary to what many people assumed, the shuttle was not completely autonomous: a trained safety operator sat in the front seat and could take control with a single button, although in practice she touched the wheel only four times during the first month.\n\nThe vehicle was equipped with six cameras, three radar sensors and a roof-mounted laser scanner that measured distances to objects up to two hundred metres away. Rakhimova explained that no single sensor was reliable enough on its own. Cameras struggle in strong sunlight, radar cannot read road signs, and lasers are confused by heavy rain. Only by combining the three could the software build a picture dependable enough for a busy street. Nevertheless, the first week revealed problems nobody had predicted. The shuttle stopped abruptly whenever a flock of pigeons landed in front of it, and it hesitated for almost a minute at a junction where a street vendor regularly pushed a cart across the road without looking. Engineers adjusted the software so that it could distinguish between birds and obstacles, but the cart remained a challenge until the vendor, Bahodir, agreed to use a marked crossing.\n\nPublic opinion was divided. Supporters pointed out that human error causes the vast majority of accidents, and they argued that a machine never gets tired or distracted by a phone. Critics, among them a retired taxi driver named Anvar, worried about the consequences for people who earn their living behind the wheel. 'A shuttle is harmless today,' he said, 'but nobody knows what happens when there are a thousand of them.' Rakhimova did not dismiss this concern. She admitted that automation would eventually change some jobs, yet she insisted that the technology was far from ready to replace drivers in the near future, since it still could not cope with unmarked rural roads or sudden road works.\n\nBy the end of the trial, the shuttle had carried 3150 passengers without a single collision. A survey showed that sixty-eight percent of them felt safe, while only nine percent said they would never ride in one again. The council has not yet decided whether to make the service permanent. A final report is due in September, and a vote will take place in October. Meanwhile, Rakhimova's team plans to test a larger vehicle on the road to the airport, which is nearly twelve kilometres long, provided the budget is approved. She hopes that, by the time she is fifty, driverless public transport will seem as ordinary as the tram once did.",
