    topic: "A coding bootcamp",
    passage: "Zarina Yusupova had worked as a pharmacy assistant in Namangan for six years when she decided, at the age of twenty-nine, to attend a coding bootcamp in Tashkent. The programme lasted twenty-six weeks, cost the equivalent of four thousand dollars, and promised that graduates would be ready for junior developer positions. Her friends were sceptical. Her cousin Jasur, who had studied computer science at university for four years, told her that bootcamps were only a clever way of selling hope to people in a hurry. Zarina listened politely, but she had already made up her mind.\n\nThe first month was harder than she had expected. Classes started at nine in the morning and finished at six, but the real work began in the evening, when students were supposed to complete exercises that often took until midnight. Of the thirty-two people who began the course, five dropped out before the end of the second week. Zarina nearly joined them. She later admitted that she cried twice in the first fortnight, not because the material was impossible, but because everybody around her seemed to understand it faster. Her instructor, a patient man named Timur, noticed her frustration and advised her to stop comparing herself with others. 'Programming is not a race,' he told the group. 'Nobody remembers who finished the exercise first, but everybody remembers who gave up.'\n\nGradually, things improved. By the tenth week Zarina could build a simple website, and by the twentieth she was working in a team of four on a booking application for a local dentist. This project taught her something that no textbook could: that most of a programmer's time is spent not writing new code but reading and fixing code written by other people. She also discovered that she was much better at testing software than her classmates, because years in the pharmacy had trained her to check details carefully.\n\nGraduation took place in early June, and the results were mixed. Eighteen of the twenty-seven graduates found jobs within three months, but Zarina was not among the first. She sent out forty applications and received only six replies, mostly because employers wanted at least a year of experience. In the end it was her dentist project that changed everything. The dentist recommended her to his brother, who ran a small logistics company, and she was hired as a junior tester in September on a salary lower than she had earned at the pharmacy.\n\nLooking back, Zarina has a balanced view. She does not believe that a bootcamp is a shortcut, and she would not recommend one to anyone unwilling to sacrifice evenings and weekends. Still, she considers it the best decision of her adult life. 'My cousin was partly right,' she says with a smile. 'Nobody sold me hope. They sold me a chance, and I had to do the rest myself.'",
