    topic: "A lab accident that led to a discovery",
    passage: "On a rainy Thursday in November 2014, Dr. Ingrid Solheim, a materials scientist at a small university laboratory in Bergen, made a mistake that she now describes as the most useful of her career. She was testing a new type of plastic film designed to protect fruit during transport, and she needed to heat a mixture of seaweed extract and a resin to exactly eighty degrees. Distracted by a phone call from her daughter's school, she forgot the beaker on the hotplate and left the building. When she returned, almost two hours later, the temperature had reached one hundred and forty degrees, and the mixture had turned into a dark, rubbery sheet that stuck to the glass.\n\nMost researchers would have thrown it away. Solheim, however, noticed that the sheet had an unusual property: when she pulled it apart, it slowly joined together again, as if it were healing. Her assistant, a cautious young man named Eskil, suggested that the effect was probably caused by moisture in the air and would disappear by the next morning. It did not. Over the following weeks the team cut the material into pieces, left them under different conditions, and measured how quickly the cuts closed. At room temperature the repair took six hours, but when the material was warmed to forty degrees it took less than twenty minutes.\n\nThe discovery would have been of little interest, Solheim says, if the sheet had been weak. In fact, it was surprisingly strong, and it was made almost entirely from waste seaweed, which coastal factories normally pay to dispose of. Within a year the group had published a paper, and within two a company that makes protective cases for mobile phones had asked to buy a licence. Not everyone was enthusiastic. A senior colleague warned that the public might wrongly conclude that accidents are a reliable method of research, and that young scientists would stop being careful. Solheim partly agrees. 'Luck only helps people who are paying attention afterwards,' she says. 'Had Eskil and I ignored that sheet, the accident would have been just an expensive stain.'\n\nThe laboratory has since changed its safety rules, not because of the accident itself but because Solheim left a hotplate unattended. Every heating device now has an automatic timer that switches it off after ninety minutes. Ironically, she admits, the timer would have prevented the discovery. She has framed a photograph of the original dark sheet and hung it above her desk, next to a handwritten note that reads, in Norwegian, 'Look twice at what you wanted to throw away.' Her next project, she says, will be a version of the material that can be recycled completely, a goal that her department expects to take at least three more years.",
