    topic: "Social media algorithms",
    passage: "Every evening, millions of people open an app 'just to check one thing' and look up forty minutes later, wondering where the time has gone. This is not an accident, nor is it a sign of weak character. According to Nodira Karimova, a data analyst who spent five years working for a social media company before resigning in 2021, it is the predictable result of systems designed to maximise one number: the time users spend on the platform.\n\nThe algorithms that decide what appears in your feed do not understand quality, truth or kindness. They understand behaviour. If you pause for three seconds on a video about cats, you will see more videos about cats; if you argue in the comments under a political post, you will see more political posts, usually the angriest ones, because anger keeps people engaged longer than agreement does. Karimova points out that companies rarely lie about this. What they avoid saying is that the system is not neutral, and that it quietly shapes opinions by deciding which voices are heard and which disappear.\n\nDefenders of the platforms offer a different view. They argue that algorithms simply give people what they ask for, and that blaming a recommendation system for human curiosity is like blaming a library for the books it lends. There is some force in this argument, Karimova concedes, but it ignores a crucial difference: a librarian does not follow you home, rearrange the shelves every time you look away, and send notifications at midnight. Moreover, a library does not earn money from the number of hours you remain inside it.\n\nThe consequences are most serious for teenagers. A study of nine hundred students in four Uzbek regions found that those who spent more than three hours daily on short-video apps were twice as likely to report difficulty concentrating in class. The researchers were careful, however, to avoid claiming that the apps caused the problem; it is equally possible that students who already struggle with concentration are drawn to such content. Karimova considers this honesty typical of good science, and she regrets that headlines rarely show the same caution.\n\nSo what can be done? She does not recommend deleting every account, which she considers unrealistic. Instead, she suggests three habits: turning off notifications, choosing manually whom to follow rather than relying on suggestions, and setting a fixed time limit on the phone itself instead of trusting willpower. She also supports legislation that would force companies to offer a chronological feed as the default option. 'People should be able to choose,' she says, 'but at the moment the choice is made for them, and they do not even notice.' Whether governments will act soon remains uncertain, since regulators are often several years behind the technology they are trying to control.",
