    topic: "A space telescope",
    passage: "When the Meridian Space Telescope was launched in a rocket from French Guiana on the ninth of December, its designers held their breath for twenty-nine days. That was how long it took for the instrument to travel one and a half million kilometres to its final position, a point in space where the gravity of the Sun and Earth together keeps it in a stable place. Once there, it had to unfold a mirror made of eighteen separate hexagonal segments, a procedure that involved three hundred and forty points of potential failure. Engineers often joke that the mission's real name should have been 'Nothing Goes Wrong'.\n\nNothing did go wrong, but the telescope was not an instant success in the public imagination. Its first image, released in July, showed a tiny patch of sky no larger than a grain of sand held at arm's length, and many viewers expected something more dramatic. Dr. Aigerim Beisenova, an astrophysicist from Almaty who has worked on the project since 2012, understands that disappointment. 'People imagine planets and glowing clouds,' she says, 'but this picture contains thousands of galaxies, some of them more than twelve billion years old. The light we see left them long before the Earth existed.'\n\nThe telescope observes in infrared light, which means that it can see through dust clouds that block ordinary telescopes, but it also means that it must be kept extremely cold. A sunshield the size of a tennis court protects the instruments, keeping them below minus two hundred and twenty degrees. If the shield were damaged, the telescope's own heat would overwhelm the faint signals it is trying to detect.\n\nThe cost of the project has been a source of controversy. The original budget was one billion dollars and the planned launch date was 2007, but delays and redesigns pushed the final cost to almost ten billion and the launch fourteen years later than planned. Critics argue that the money could have funded dozens of smaller missions, which would have produced more varied results. Supporters respond that no smaller instrument could have detected the faintest galaxies, and that the technology developed along the way, such as new methods for polishing mirrors, has already been adopted by hospitals and manufacturers.\n\nThe most exciting work, according to Beisenova, concerns the atmospheres of distant planets. By studying starlight as it passes through a planet's air, scientists can identify gases such as water vapour and carbon dioxide. Within the first year, the team detected clouds on a planet nearly two hundred light years away. This does not mean that life has been found, and Beisenova is quick to correct journalists who suggest it. 'Finding water is exciting, but it is only one ingredient,' she says. 'We would need several signs at once before anyone could responsibly claim a discovery.'\n\nThe telescope has enough fuel to operate for about twenty years, though its mirrors will probably remain useful much longer. Beisenova hopes that by then a successor will exist, and she tells her students that the person who designs it may be sitting in their classroom today.",
