    topic: "Data privacy",
    passage: "Last month my grandmother, Gulchehra, who is seventy-three and has never owned anything more complicated than a basic mobile phone, received a call from a stranger who knew her full name, her address and the name of her local clinic. The caller claimed to be from the health ministry and offered a free check-up if she would confirm her passport number. Fortunately, she hung up and rang my father. I tell this story not because it is unusual but because it is typical: the information used against her was not stolen in a dramatic hack, but gathered from dozens of small, legal sources.\n\nMost of us believe that privacy means keeping secrets, and since we have 'nothing to hide', we shrug our shoulders when apps ask for permission to access our contacts, location or microphone. This is a misunderstanding. Privacy is not about hiding wrongdoing; it is about controlling who knows what about you, and for what purpose. You do not mind your doctor knowing your medical history, but you would certainly mind your employer or your insurance company knowing it.\n\nThe problem is that data rarely stays where it was collected. A free fitness app records your daily route, and sells the anonymised data to an advertising broker. The broker combines it with shopping records from a loyalty card and a map of your neighbourhood. 'Anonymised' sounds reassuring, yet researchers have shown that four locations visited at known times are enough to identify ninety-five percent of individuals in a large dataset. In other words, removing a name protects far less than companies imply.\n\nSome experts insist that the solution lies in stronger legislation, and several countries have already introduced rules requiring companies to delete personal data upon request and to explain, in plain language, what they collect. Others are less optimistic. They point out that fines are often smaller than the profits made from the violation, so that breaking the law becomes simply a cost of doing business. My own view is that laws are necessary but insufficient; individuals must also learn to protect themselves.\n\nHere are the habits I now teach my family. First, read the permission list before installing an app, and refuse anything that has no obvious connection to the app's purpose: a torch does not need your contacts. Second, use a different password for every important account, and keep them in a password manager rather than a notebook. Third, never confirm personal details to someone who has called you; instead, end the conversation and phone the organisation yourself using the number on its official website. My grandmother has now written that last rule on a card beside her telephone, and, she says, she is rather proud of it. Not every threat can be avoided, but most can be made much harder to carry out.",
