    topic: "A smart home",
    passage: "When Rustam Hasanov, a thirty-eight-year-old accountant in Bukhara, bought a smart home package for his new flat, he imagined that his life would become effortlessly comfortable. The kit, which cost about six hundred dollars, included a thermostat, four light bulbs, a door lock, two cameras and a voice assistant that sat on the kitchen shelf like a small black bowl. The salesman had promised that everything would be connected within an hour. In reality, it took Rustam an entire Saturday, three phone calls to customer support and one argument with his wife, Shahlo, who suspected that he cared more about the gadgets than about the weekend they had planned.\n\nThe first weeks were delightful. The lights turned on as he walked into the corridor, the heating lowered itself when the flat was empty, and the thermostat learned, after ten days, that the family liked the bedroom cooler at night. Their electricity bill fell by roughly eleven percent in the first quarter. Rustam could also check the cameras from his office, and he admits that he did so more often than necessary.\n\nProblems began in the second month. One evening the internet connection failed, and the door lock, which depended on it, refused to open for Shahlo, who stood outside with two heavy bags and a crying toddler. Rustam, stuck in traffic across the city, could do nothing except advise her to knock on a neighbour's door. 'A machine that is supposed to simplify life must never become a reason for locking your own family out,' he says. The next day he bought a traditional key and installed it beside the electronic one.\n\nThere were more subtle issues too. The voice assistant sometimes started to answer when nobody had spoken to it, and Shahlo noticed that advertisements for baby clothes appeared on her phone shortly after she had mentioned the subject in the kitchen. Whether the device was actually listening to conversations or whether it was merely coincidence, nobody could say with certainty, but the feeling was uncomfortable enough that she asked Rustam to disconnect its microphone when guests visited.\n\nToday the family uses about half of the original equipment. The thermostat and the bulbs remain, because they provide clear savings, whereas the cameras were moved to the front door only and the voice assistant is used mainly as a kitchen timer. Rustam does not regret the purchase, but he would give different advice to friends. 'Start with one device that solves a real problem,' he says. 'Buy others only after you have lived with the first one for a few months. And always keep a key in your pocket.'",
