    topic: "A robot cafe",
    passage: "In the centre of Almaty, on the ground floor of an old office building, there is a café where the barista never says good morning, never takes a day off and never spills a drop. The barista is an industrial robot arm named Zhanna, installed in September by a young entrepreneur, Daniyar Omarov, after he spent a year watching how his own café, staffed by humans, wasted time and money. 'I did not build it to be fashionable,' he insists. 'I built it because on busy mornings I had a queue of forty people and only three workers on shift.'\n\nZhanna makes a cup of coffee in about forty seconds, which is slightly slower than a trained human, but she can do it two hundred times a day without any variation in quality. Customers order on a screen, pay by card, and watch through a glass wall as the arm selects a cup, grinds the beans, steams the milk and slides the drink onto a counter. Many take a photograph before drinking. The café attracted so much attention on social media in its first fortnight that sales rose by sixty percent, although Omarov admits that much of this was curiosity rather than loyalty. 'Novelty brings people once,' he says. 'Only good coffee brings them back.'\n\nThe robot has not replaced the staff, contrary to the fears of local residents. Omarov employs five people, the same number as before, but their work has changed. Instead of making drinks, they greet customers, clean tables, prepare sandwiches and help elderly visitors who find the screen confusing. One of them, Madina, a student who works weekends, says she was initially afraid of losing her job. 'Now I enjoy it more,' she says, 'because I talk to people instead of repeating the same movements all day. But I do not think every manager would be as generous as Daniyar.'\n\nHer scepticism is shared by economists. Professor Kairat Seitov of the local university warns that a café with a robot is, in the short term, an expensive experiment: the machine cost the equivalent of forty-five thousand dollars and will need about three years to repay itself. Large chains, however, could save much more by installing hundreds of machines, and in that case employees might not be as fortunate as Omarov's. 'The technology itself is neither good nor bad,' Seitov says. 'The consequences depend on the decisions of owners, and on what governments require them to do for the workers they replace.'\n\nNot everything works perfectly. During a power cut in November, Zhanna froze with a full cup of hot milk in her gripper, and the staff had to wait twenty minutes before they could safely remove it. A customer also discovered that the robot cannot draw patterns in the milk foam, something that most human baristas learn in their first month. Omarov is working with the manufacturer to correct this, and he hopes that by next spring his robot will produce a heart or a flower on request, although he knows that some customers will always prefer a smile to a pattern.",
