    topic: "An inventor's biography",
    passage: "Abdulla Nazarov was born in 1921 in a village near Kokand, the fifth of nine children in a family of apricot growers. As a boy he noticed something that most adults overlooked: nearly a third of the fruit his family harvested rotted before it could be sold, because the traditional method of drying it on rooftops depended on the weather and attracted flies. He left school at fourteen to work in a repair workshop, but he continued to read whatever technical books he could borrow, including, to his surprise, an old textbook on physics that a teacher had thrown away.\n\nHis first invention, built in 1948 when he was twenty-seven, was a wooden box with a sloping glass lid and a black metal tray inside. Fruit placed on the tray dried in four days instead of eight, and, because the box was closed, insects could not reach it. Nazarov did not patent the design. He made forty copies by hand and gave most of them to neighbours, believing that an idea kept secret was a wasted idea. Some historians regard this generosity as admirable; others consider it naive, since a company in another country later sold a nearly identical dryer for profit and never mentioned him.\n\nThe turning point of his career came in 1957, when a visiting agricultural inspector, Valentina Orlova, saw the dryers in use and wrote a report recommending that the ministry support their production. Nazarov was invited to Tashkent, where he spent three years improving the design with a team of engineers. The new version could dry ten times as much fruit, and it included a small fan powered by a solar panel, a feature he had suggested but initially been told was impractical. Within a decade, more than eight thousand dryers were in use across Central Asia, and the proportion of fruit lost after harvest in some regions dropped from thirty percent to under ten.\n\nNazarov never became wealthy, and he seemed to care little about that. He lived in the same modest house for fifty years, and when he was offered a post as director of a research institute, he refused, explaining that he would be happier in a workshop than behind a desk. His students, however, remember that he was far from sentimental about his work. He insisted that every device be tested for at least a year before it was recommended to anyone, and he once destroyed a prototype in front of them because it broke twice during trials.\n\nHe died in 1996, aged seventy-five. Today a small museum in Kokand displays his first wooden box, which still works, and a quotation attributed to him is painted above the entrance: 'A good invention is one that a poor man can build and a rich man cannot improve.'",
