    topic: "A museum of science",
    passage: "I had expected the new science museum in Samarkand to be a collection of dusty glass cases and labels in tiny print, which is why I was astonished when my twelve-year-old nephew, Oybek, who normally measures any outing by how soon he can return to his phone, asked to stay beyond the closing time. The museum opened in April in a converted cotton warehouse, and it deliberately breaks several traditions. There are almost no objects that visitors are forbidden to touch, and the director, Dr. Feruza Najmiddinova, a former physics teacher, told me that she measures success not by the number of tickets sold but by the number of questions children ask on the way out.\n\nThe building has four floors, each devoted to a different theme. The ground floor, called Motion, contains a tall tower where visitors can drop objects of different weights and watch them fall through a transparent tube from which the air has been pumped out; a feather and a metal ball reach the bottom at the same moment, a sight that Oybek made me watch six times. The second floor explores light and sound, and includes a dark room where voices are turned into coloured patterns on the wall. The third floor, on the human body, was the least popular with my nephew, though I found its model of a beating heart, big enough to walk through, quite moving. The fourth floor is a workshop where, on weekends, anyone can build and test small machines under the guidance of volunteer engineers.\n\nNot everything impressed me. The queue at the entrance was nearly forty minutes long on Saturday, and the café offers only sandwiches that I would not recommend. More seriously, several of the explanations are written for adults with a university education rather than for the children for whom the museum seems to be designed. Standing in front of a display about electricity, I overheard a boy ask his father what 'resistance' meant, and the father, apparently an electrician, answered correctly but too quickly for the boy to follow.\n\nA more subtle criticism concerns the museum's name for its approach, 'learning by doing'. While hands-on activities are certainly entertaining, there is a danger that visitors leave remembering the fun but not the principle behind it. Najmiddinova accepts this risk. She says that curiosity must come first and understanding later, and that a child who has dropped a feather and a ball will one day, perhaps in a school lesson, recall the experience and finally grasp why the feather fell more slowly outside the tube.\n\nTickets cost about three dollars for children and six for adults, and on the first Wednesday of each month entry is free for school groups. If you plan to go, arrive before ten in the morning, wear comfortable shoes and allow at least three hours. Oybek, for the record, is already asking when we can return.",
