    topic: "A school robotics team",
    passage: "When the robotics club at School Number Forty-Four in Urgench was founded four years ago, it had five members, one broken laptop and a teacher who admitted that he knew less about robots than most of his students. Today, it has twenty-two members, a proper workshop and a trophy cabinet with, by the teacher's own account, more dust than trophies. The club's coach, Mr. Otabek Yuldashev, a mathematics teacher, says that this modest record is exactly what he is proud of: 'Our aim has never been to win. Our aim is to produce students who can solve a problem that nobody has explained to them yet.'\n\nIn October the team travelled eleven hours by train to Tashkent for the national school competition, where teams had six weeks to build a robot that could collect plastic bottles from a floor and sort them by colour. The favourites were two schools from the capital with expensive equipment and sponsors. Urgench had a budget of about two hundred dollars and a design that used a camera taken from an old mobile phone. Captain Madina Sobirova, sixteen, explains that the cheapness forced them to be creative: 'We could not buy a good sensor, so we taught the software to recognise colours ourselves. In the end, that was the part that the judges liked most.'\n\nThe competition did not go smoothly. During the first round, the robot's left wheel came loose, and the team lost nine of its twenty allowed minutes while fixing it with a hairpin borrowed from a girl in the audience. The robot completed the task only partially, collecting seven bottles out of fifteen. Many teams would have been discouraged, but the Urgench students used the break between rounds to redesign the wheel mount, and in the second round they collected fourteen bottles, the second-highest score of the day.\n\nThey finished third overall, because the judges also awarded points for teamwork and for a presentation about the design process. Sobirova admits that she was disappointed for a few minutes, since she had hoped for first place, but she changed her mind when an engineer from a technology company approached the team and offered each member a place on a summer programme. 'I realised that nobody had offered us that because of a medal,' she says. 'They offered it because of how we behaved when the wheel fell off.'\n\nBack in Urgench, the club is now planning a different kind of project. Instead of a competition robot, they intend to build a simple machine that waters plants in the school garden according to the moisture in the soil. Yuldashev supports the idea warmly, though he hesitates to call it a step down. 'A machine that is actually used every day teaches more than a machine that is used for twenty minutes on stage,' he says. 'And the younger students, who are only eleven or twelve, will be able to take part from the beginning.'",
