    topic: "An AI translator",
    passage: "Sherzod Tojiev, a tour guide in Khiva, still remembers the day a group of visitors from Japan stood in front of him in silence for almost a minute because neither side could find a word the other understood. Ten years later, the same scene would be unlikely, since most of his clients now hold phones that translate speech almost instantly. Yet Sherzod, who speaks four languages himself, is not convinced that the technology is as wonderful as its advertisements suggest.\n\nModern translation software works in a way that would surprise most users. Instead of looking up each word in a dictionary, as early programs did, it has studied billions of sentences in many languages and learned which patterns usually correspond to each other. This is why the results are often fluent, even elegant. However, fluency is not the same thing as accuracy. Because the program predicts what a sentence ought to look like, it occasionally produces a smooth sentence that says something different from the original. Linguist Dr. Gavhar Rahmonova gives an example from a hospital in Tashkent, where a program turned a doctor's note saying 'no known allergies' into a sentence suggesting that the patient was allergic to several medicines.\n\nIdioms create further difficulties. When an Uzbek speaker says that something is 'as easy as eating plov', the software may translate it literally, leaving the foreigner confused, or it may replace it with an English phrase of its own, such as 'a piece of cake', which is accurate in meaning yet loses the cultural flavour. Rahmonova considers the second solution better for business, but the first more interesting for literature, and she says that no program can choose correctly between them without understanding the purpose of the text.\n\nSupporters point out that the technology has transformed the lives of immigrants, refugees and small traders, who can now communicate in situations where a professional interpreter would be unaffordable. Sherzod grants this but warns about dependence. He has noticed that some young guides have stopped learning languages altogether, assuming that the phone will do the work. 'A translator can give you words,' he says, 'but not a relationship. When I tell a joke in a visitor's own language, even badly, they laugh because I made an effort. A phone cannot make an effort on my behalf.'\n\nRahmonova offers a practical compromise. Use the machine for everyday needs such as menus, timetables and directions, where a small error causes little harm, but turn to a human professional for contracts, medical advice and legal documents, where a single wrong word may have serious consequences. She adds that the best users of translation software are people who know something of the language, because they can notice when a sentence feels strange. Sherzod, for his part, has begun to learn Korean, in the expectation that Korean visitors will outnumber Japanese ones within five years. He does not rely on his phone for lessons. 'I use it,' he says, 'only to check that my teacher is not laughing at me.'",
